Third-party Go dependencies
---------------------------

The interactive interface is built on Charm's Bubble Tea and Lip Gloss, which
are fetched as ordinary Go modules rather than vendored here. Everything else
GoshCoder does — provider requests, SSE parsing, AWS SigV4, archive handling,
the cross-process lock — is written against the Go standard library.

A Go binary statically links its dependencies, so a release archive has to
carry their notices. `make dist` collects the licence text of every module
that survives into the build — for that platform specifically, from
`go list -deps` rather than from go.mod — into a `licenses/` directory beside
the binary, with `licenses/modules.txt` listing each module and version. A
dependency with no discoverable licence fails the build rather than shipping
silently; see scripts/collect-licenses.sh.

Building from source instead? The same texts are in your module cache, at the
paths `go list -m -f '{{.Dir}}' <module>` prints. go.sum is not a substitute:
it records versions and integrity hashes, not licences.

One dependency needs a note. github.com/mattn/go-localereader, reached through
Bubble Tea on Windows, ships no licence file and declares MIT in its README
instead. The text carried in its place is recorded by hand under
scripts/license-exceptions/ so the notice is auditable rather than inferred.


